  "result": "If I could flip a single, world-sized switch, I\u2019d give every person an unshakable, firsthand sense of one another\u2019s inner lives\u2014the joys, fears, aspirations, and vulnerabilities that make us all human.  \n\nWhy that? Because most of our largest problems\u2014violence, inequality, environmental neglect, indifference to distant suffering\u2014survive on a kind of imaginative poverty. When we fail to feel how real other people are, it becomes easy to treat them as abstractions, enemies, or expendable costs. The moment that distance collapses, cooperation stops being a moral chore and starts feeling like enlightened self-interest.\n\nA surge of authentic empathy wouldn\u2019t guarantee utopia; hard decisions and conflicts would remain. But policies about war, climate, economics, or justice would be debated in a fundamentally different emotional atmosphere\u2014one where it\u2019s harder to ignore the stakes for someone else. With that single change, every other improvement we hope for would have sturdier ground to stand on.",
